# Copy to .env and fill in — see docs/SECURITY.md §14-15.
# Expo only exposes vars prefixed EXPO_PUBLIC_ to client code. These are
# the anon/public Supabase key — safe to ship because every table it can
# touch is protected by Row Level Security (see supabase/migrations).
# NEVER put a Supabase service-role key in this app.

# Local Supabase (after `supabase start`): http://127.0.0.1:54321
#
# IMPORTANT — testing on a physical device via Expo Go/a dev client:
# `127.0.0.1`/`localhost` will NOT work. On a real phone that address
# means the phone itself, not your computer, so it can never reach a
# Supabase instance running on your machine — every sign-in/sign-up/
# password-reset call fails at the network level before it ever reaches
# Supabase, and (see lib/auth/errors.ts) that currently surfaces as the
# same generic "Something went wrong" message auth shows for any other
# failure. Use your computer's LAN IP instead (e.g. `ipconfig getifaddr
# en0` on macOS, `hostname -I` on Linux, `ipconfig` on Windows) — e.g.
# http://192.168.1.23:54321 — or run `supabase start` with a public URL
# (see the Supabase CLI docs) if your device isn't on the same network.
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
# Local Supabase: the "anon key" printed by `supabase start`
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
